termined  by the random values of those elements  our procedure for this problem is simply  to probe some set of elements  f such that that element that set is  feasible in the constraint  and our goal is to maximize the expected  value of u of f  now we can consider two different types  of strategies for this  problem adaptive strategies which is the  general case  can choose the next element to probe  based on  all of our knowledge from previous  probes  on the other hand we have much more  restrictive non-adaptive strategies  that have to choose the set of elements  they're going to probe in advance  and then they commit to probing all of  the ones that they chose  the adaptivity gap beta measures  the worst case ratio of these two  quantities  the optimal non-adaptive expected value  to the optimal adaptive expected value  and this answers the question  what fraction of the optimal expected  value can we get  when we're only using a non-adaptive  strategy  now we can use the adaptivity gap along  with our previous result  to get delegation gaps for delegation  problems with inner and other  constraints  the basic idea here is to define the  function u  to be the optimal utility that the  non-delegating principle gets  when they probe exactly the elements f  then we can use the adaptivity gap for  the function u  on the constraint on our outer  constraint to find  a set f feasible and add a constraint  that approximately maximizes  u now given the set f  we can consider the restricted  delegation instance  restricted to only the elements in f and  then because  f is feasible in the other constraint  the other constraint is no longer  relevant it no longer  restrains us so now we can use this  to run our previous mechanism which  works  when our delegation problem has no outer  constraint and only  an inner constraint and this is the  basic idea  formalizing this intuition we get the  following theorem  which says that there's a reduction from  delegation problems  that removes the outer constraint losing  only a factor of the adaptivity gap of  weighted rank functions of inner  constraint  on the outer constraint so we lose only  the factor of an adaptivity gap to  remove  the other constraint  as the corollary of this result along  with our previous corollary  we get that there are constant factor  strategies for delegation  for delegation problems with any  downward closed outer constraint  and an inner constraint that's the  intersection of a constant number  of metroids this follows from the work  of bradak singla and zuzik  finally we briefly investigate lottery  mechanisms which are a type of  randomized mechanism  the procedure here is very similar to  our deterministic mechanisms from before  with one slight change the principle  starts by committing to a menu of  distributions of the solutions  rather than before they commit to a menu  over deterministic solutions  in response the agent will still probe  as usual  and then propose one distribution from  the menu rather than proposing  a solution the principal finally  accepts a sample from that distribution  that the agent proposed  we're interested in comparing how how  good  lottery mechanisms are when compared to  deterministic mechanisms  and we do so in the special case of no  outer constraint  and the one uniform metroid in your  constraint  we show that there are some instances  where lottery mechanisms  have a delegation gap of one or  essentially one  whereas deterministic mechanisms have a  gap of one-half  so lottery mechanisms perform much  better  but in some worst-case instances both  mechanisms have a gap  of one-half and this is tight and this  provides evidence that lottery  mechanisms  do not do better than deterministic ones  in the worst case  i'll leave you with some open questions  from our work  first off are these factors tight we  give a bunch of constant factors but  we only know one that's tight in  particular we know that one half is  tight  for instances with no outer constraint  and the one uniform metroid inner  constraint  and this is true even when we're using  lottery mechanisms  but for other instances we don't know  we can ask how hard it is to compute  these strategies  what computational complexity does it  require  to compute the strategies we described  what if each element has a probing cost  instead of or in addition to the outer  constraint and this is inspired  by the binary model of climb breaking  kleinberg  well if the principal can incentivize  the agent with payments  if they pay for preferable solutions  can the principal do better than without  paying  and what if the principal delegates to  multiple agents simultaneously  can they get those agents to compete  with each other  and get a better outcome for themselves  with that i'll leave you there and thank  you for  listening
 

